From ca893fb88d1a064393b83ff7a8c8b3fb2f7b8088 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:19:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 37/37] cmos: publish the host's wall clock, instead of zeroes

The first container Boks ran end to end on Windows 11 reported

    uptime  139.62 1110.64
    date -u Tue Nov 30 00:02:19 UTC 1999   (host: 2026-08-14T14:58:07Z)

and every HTTPS request out of that guest failed with "curl (60) SSL
certificate problem: certificate is not yet valid". Nothing that fetches
a dependency, clones over HTTPS or calls an API can work in a guest whose
clock says 1999.

1999-11-30 is not a plausible clock reading. It is not the epoch, not the
1980 a dead CMOS battery gives you, and 139.62 s of uptime says the guest
had been running for over two minutes when it printed 00:02:19 -- so the
value it started from was 1999-11-30T00:00:00Z exactly, or 943_920_000.
That constant is reachable in one way. Linux reads this device in
mach_get_cmos_time() (arch/x86/kernel/rtc.c), which with CONFIG_ACPI off
-- so with no FADT century register to consult -- finishes:

    year += CMOS_YEARS_OFFS;               /* 2000 */
    now->tv_sec = mktime64(year, mon, day, hour, min, sec);

An all-zero register file is year 0, month 0, day 0, and mktime64() does
not validate: month 0 rolls back to December and day 0 to the day before
the 1st, so mktime64(2000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) == 943_920_000. The guest had
not read a clock that was wrong. It had read a device that has never had
a clock in it at all -- Cmos::new() zeroed 128 bytes, wrote the two
memory-size fields, and left every time register at zero forever.

Nothing about that is Windows-specific, and it is worth being explicit
that the device has been broken on every platform since it was added. It
was invisible because until the WHP backend existed no guest ever read
these registers:

  * Linux/KVM. The guest kernel is CONFIG_KVM_GUEST=y and
    CONFIG_PARAVIRT_CLOCK=y. kvmclock_init() runs from setup_arch(),
    before timekeeping_init(), and points x86_platform.get_wallclock at
    kvm_get_wallclock(), which reads the host's wall clock from the
    kvmclock MSR page. mach_get_cmos_time() is never called.
  * macOS/HVF. aarch64 only, where the RTC is the PL031 in
    legacy::rtc_pl031, which has always seeded itself from
    ClockType::Real, and the aarch64 guest config has CONFIG_RTC_CLASS=y,
    CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS=y and CONFIG_RTC_DRV_PL031=y to consume it.
  * Windows/WHP. No KVM CPUID signature, therefore no kvmclock; no PIT,
    HPET or PM timer either. CMOS is the only wall clock the partition
    has.

So publish the host's wall clock: seconds through years plus the century
register at 0x32, in the BCD, 24-hour encoding status register B declares,
with UIP in status register A held clear (mach_get_cmos_time() spins on it
with no timeout) and VRT in status register D set. The date arithmetic is
Howard Hinnant's civil_from_days, and the tests decode it back through a
verbatim transcription of the kernel's own mktime64() -- checking the
device against its actual consumer rather than against a restatement of
the encoder, which is what a bug whose fingerprint is a date arithmetic
edge case deserves.

One read burst gets one instant. The guest reads seven registers on the
strength of "UIP is clear, so the time will not change for 244 us", and
each of those reads is a port-I/O VM exit handled in user mode under WHP;
computing the time per register would let a burst straddle a second
boundary and put the guest up to 59 s ahead, or -- if the tick crossed
midnight on 31 December -- in the wrong year, which is the whole problem
again. The latch ages out after 100 ms, far longer than any read burst
and far shorter than the one-second resolution the registers can express,
so the device stays a clock rather than becoming a snapshot.

Two things this deliberately does not do.

It does not accept writes to the data port; they stay ignored, as before.
A guest-settable RTC means modelling an offset from host time, and no
guest that boots here asks for one: the x86_64 config has
"# CONFIG_RTC_CLASS is not set", so there is no rtc-cmos driver to write
back through, and aarch64 talks to the PL031 instead.

It does not keep the guest in sync afterwards, and cannot. The value is
recomputed on every read, so a guest that reads repeatedly does track the
host -- but that same missing CONFIG_RTC_CLASS means this guest reads the
persistent clock exactly once, in timekeeping_init(), and thereafter
free-runs on the TSC with no /dev/rtc, no hwclock and no NTP client in the
rootfs to correct it. Boot-time accuracy is what certificate validity
needs and what a two-second container gets; long-lived guests will drift
with the TSC, and a host suspend/resume will not be caught. Closing that
gap needs a paravirtual clock or a time-sync channel, which is a much
larger change than this one and belongs with the guest image that would
have to consume it.

Setting the clock here rather than in the init is also what makes it right
at the moment userspace starts: timekeeping_init() is inside
start_kernel(), long before PID 1. An init calling clock_settime() would
be correcting a value the kernel had already sampled wrong, and would
leave everything stamped before it ran -- including anything the kernel
itself wrote -- in 1999.

UNVERIFIED: no Windows host was available, so no guest has read these
registers. What is tested, on the host, is that the register file decodes
back to the host's own clock through the kernel's arithmetic, for every
date from 2000 to 2100 and for the live clock through the BusDevice
interface the vCPU's port-I/O exit calls.
---
 src/devices/src/legacy/x86_64/cmos.rs | 420 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 418 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/devices/src/legacy/x86_64/cmos.rs b/src/devices/src/legacy/x86_64/cmos.rs
index 52223c9..cf88ed8 100644
--- a/src/devices/src/legacy/x86_64/cmos.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/legacy/x86_64/cmos.rs
@@ -1,18 +1,197 @@
 // Copyright 2025 Red Hat, Inc.
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 
+//! MC146818-compatible CMOS RAM and real-time clock.
+//!
+//! The CMOS RAM half carries the memory-size bytes a legacy BIOS would have
+//! left for the guest. The RTC half publishes the host's wall clock, which is
+//! the only channel through which an x86 guest running under a hypervisor
+//! without a paravirtual clock can learn what year it is.
+//!
+//! # Why the RTC half exists
+//!
+//! Every time register used to read back as zero, because the register file was
+//! a zeroed array that nothing ever wrote a time into. Linux consumes it in
+//! `mach_get_cmos_time()` (arch/x86/kernel/rtc.c), which — with `CONFIG_ACPI`
+//! off, so no FADT century register — ends in
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! year += CMOS_YEARS_OFFS;               /* 2000 */
+//! now->tv_sec = mktime64(year, mon, day, hour, min, sec);
+//! ```
+//!
+//! so an all-zero register file becomes `mktime64(2000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)`. Month
+//! 0 and day 0 are a month and a day *before* the base of `mktime64`'s
+//! calendar, and it computes them without complaint: 943_920_000, which is
+//! 1999-11-30T00:00:00Z. That constant is the signature of this bug — a guest
+//! reporting a date near 1999-11-30 has not read a clock that is merely wrong,
+//! it has read no clock at all.
+//!
+//! The consequence is not cosmetic. Every X.509 certificate presented to that
+//! guest has a `notBefore` in this century, so every TLS handshake fails with
+//! "certificate is not yet valid", and nothing can fetch a dependency, clone
+//! over HTTPS, or call an API.
+//!
+//! # Why nobody hit it before
+//!
+//! Nothing about the defect is Windows-specific — it is simply that until the
+//! WHP backend existed, no guest ever read these registers:
+//!
+//! * **Linux/KVM.** The guest kernel is built `CONFIG_KVM_GUEST=y` and
+//!   `CONFIG_PARAVIRT_CLOCK=y`, and `kvmclock_init()` runs from `setup_arch()`,
+//!   before `timekeeping_init()`. It replaces `x86_platform.get_wallclock` with
+//!   `kvm_get_wallclock()`, which reads the host's wall clock out of the
+//!   kvmclock MSR page. `mach_get_cmos_time()` is never called.
+//! * **macOS/HVF.** aarch64 only, where the RTC is the PL031 in
+//!   `legacy::rtc_pl031`, which has always seeded itself from
+//!   `ClockType::Real`. The aarch64 guest config has `CONFIG_RTC_CLASS=y` and
+//!   `CONFIG_RTC_DRV_PL031=y` to consume it.
+//! * **Windows/WHP.** No KVM CPUID signature, therefore no kvmclock, and the
+//!   x86_64 guest config has `# CONFIG_RTC_CLASS is not set` — there is no
+//!   `/dev/rtc`, no `rtc-cmos` driver and no `hwclock` for userspace to fall
+//!   back on. `read_persistent_clock64()` -> `mach_get_cmos_time()` at
+//!   `timekeeping_init()` is the one and only wall-clock read the guest will
+//!   ever perform, and it lands here.
+//!
+//! That last point also fixes *when* the clock has to be right: at
+//! `timekeeping_init()`, which is inside `start_kernel()` and therefore long
+//! before PID 1, let alone before a container's entrypoint. Handing the time
+//! over here is what makes it correct for a container that lives two seconds.
+//! It is equally what makes this the only place it can be handed over: an init
+//! that called `clock_settime()` would be correcting a clock the kernel had
+//! already sampled wrong, and would still leave every timestamp taken before it
+//! ran in 1999.
+
 use std::cmp::min;
+use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
 
 use crate::bus::BusDevice;
+use utils::time::{ClockType, NANOS_PER_SECOND, get_time};
 
 const INDEX_MASK: u8 = 0x7f;
 const INDEX_OFFSET: u64 = 0x0;
 const DATA_OFFSET: u64 = 0x1;
 const DATA_LEN: usize = 128;
 
+// The MC146818 time-of-day and status registers. Everything outside this set is
+// plain CMOS RAM, and is served out of `data`.
+const REG_SECONDS: u8 = 0x00;
+const REG_MINUTES: u8 = 0x02;
+const REG_HOURS: u8 = 0x04;
+const REG_WEEKDAY: u8 = 0x06;
+const REG_DAY_OF_MONTH: u8 = 0x07;
+const REG_MONTH: u8 = 0x08;
+const REG_YEAR: u8 = 0x09;
+const REG_STATUS_A: u8 = 0x0a;
+const REG_STATUS_B: u8 = 0x0b;
+const REG_STATUS_C: u8 = 0x0c;
+const REG_STATUS_D: u8 = 0x0d;
+/// The century register. Its address is not architectural — it is whatever the
+/// ACPI FADT names — but 0x32 is what every PC has used and what firmware
+/// reports, so a guest that looks for one looks here.
+const REG_CENTURY: u8 = 0x32;
+
+/// Status register A. Bit 7 (UIP, update-in-progress) is clear and stays clear;
+/// the divider and rate-select bits are the power-on defaults of a running
+/// oscillator. Reporting UIP set spuriously would be worse than useless:
+/// `mach_get_cmos_time()` spins on it with no timeout, so a stuck bit hangs the
+/// boot CPU.
+const STATUS_A: u8 = 0x26;
+/// Status register B. Bit 1 (24/12) set means 24-hour hours; bit 2 (DM) clear
+/// means the time registers are BCD, which is what a PC-compatible guest
+/// assumes and what `RTC_ALWAYS_BCD` hard-codes on x86. No interrupt-enable bit
+/// is set, because this device has no interrupt line to raise them on.
+const STATUS_B: u8 = 0x02;
+/// Status register C, the interrupt-flag register. Always zero: no periodic,
+/// alarm or update-ended interrupt is ever generated.
+const STATUS_C: u8 = 0x00;
+/// Status register D. Bit 7 (VRT, valid RAM and time) set is a battery-backed
+/// RTC saying its contents are trustworthy. The old zeroed register file
+/// reported the opposite — dead battery — while still handing out a date, which
+/// is precisely the combination a guest is entitled to disbelieve.
+const STATUS_D: u8 = 0x80;
+
+/// How long a latched calendar keeps being served before the host clock is
+/// sampled again. See [`Cmos::calendar`].
+const LATCH_WINDOW: Duration = Duration::from_millis(100);
+
+/// A broken-down UTC calendar, in binary. Converted to BCD only on the way out.
+///
+/// UTC, not local time, because that is the interpretation the guest applies:
+/// `read_persistent_clock64()` feeds `mach_get_cmos_time()`'s result straight
+/// into the timekeeper as seconds since the epoch, with no timezone step. A
+/// microVM has no CMOS bit to negotiate that in, so the choice has to be made
+/// here and stated.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]
+struct Calendar {
+    second: u8,
+    minute: u8,
+    hour: u8,
+    /// 1 = Sunday, as the MC146818 numbers it.
+    weekday: u8,
+    day: u8,
+    month: u8,
+    /// Year within the century, 0..=99.
+    year: u8,
+    /// Century, e.g. 20 for 2026.
+    century: u8,
+}
+
+/// Splits seconds since the Unix epoch into a UTC calendar.
+///
+/// The date half is Howard Hinnant's `civil_from_days`, which shifts the era to
+/// start on 1 March so that the leap day falls at the end of a 400-year cycle
+/// and needs no special case. It is exact for every date this device can
+/// express, which is what matters here: the fingerprint of the bug being fixed
+/// is a date arithmetic edge case, and replacing it with a second one would be
+/// a poor trade.
+fn calendar_from_unix(secs: u64) -> Calendar {
+    let days = (secs / 86_400) as i64;
+    let secs_of_day = secs % 86_400;
+
+    // 719_468 is the number of days from 0000-03-01 to 1970-01-01, so `z` is
+    // days since the start of the era containing the epoch.
+    let z = days + 719_468;
+    let era = z.div_euclid(146_097);
+    let day_of_era = z.rem_euclid(146_097); // [0, 146_096]
+    let year_of_era =
+        (day_of_era - day_of_era / 1_460 + day_of_era / 36_524 - day_of_era / 146_096) / 365; // [0, 399]
+    let day_of_year = day_of_era - (365 * year_of_era + year_of_era / 4 - year_of_era / 100); // [0, 365]
+    let month_prime = (5 * day_of_year + 2) / 153; // [0, 11], March = 0
+    let day = day_of_year - (153 * month_prime + 2) / 5 + 1; // [1, 31]
+    let month = if month_prime < 10 {
+        month_prime + 3
+    } else {
+        month_prime - 9
+    };
+    // The era starts in March, so January and February belong to the next year.
+    let year = year_of_era + era * 400 + i64::from(month <= 2);
+
+    Calendar {
+        second: (secs_of_day % 60) as u8,
+        minute: (secs_of_day / 60 % 60) as u8,
+        hour: (secs_of_day / 3_600) as u8,
+        // 1970-01-01 was a Thursday, and the register numbers Sunday as 1,
+        // which puts Thursday at 5.
+        weekday: ((days + 4).rem_euclid(7) + 1) as u8,
+        day: day as u8,
+        month: month as u8,
+        year: (year % 100) as u8,
+        century: (year / 100) as u8,
+    }
+}
+
+/// Encodes a value in the range 0..=99 as packed BCD, the format status
+/// register B advertises.
+fn bin_to_bcd(val: u8) -> u8 {
+    ((val / 10) << 4) | (val % 10)
+}
+
 pub struct Cmos {
     index: u8,
     data: [u8; DATA_LEN],
+    /// The calendar currently being published, and the instant it was sampled.
+    latch: Option<(Instant, Calendar)>,
 }
 
 impl Cmos {
@@ -35,7 +214,68 @@ impl Cmos {
         data[0x5c] = (high_mem >> 8) as u8;
         data[0x5d] = (high_mem >> 16) as u8;
 
-        Cmos { index: 0, data }
+        Cmos {
+            index: 0,
+            data,
+            latch: None,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Returns the calendar to publish, sampling the host clock if the latched
+    /// one has aged out.
+    ///
+    /// The contract a guest relies on is "while UIP is clear, the time
+    /// registers will not change for at least 244 µs" — `mach_get_cmos_time()`
+    /// waits for UIP to clear and then reads seven registers on the strength of
+    /// it. We report UIP permanently clear, so we owe the guest that stability,
+    /// and we cannot honour it by sampling the clock per register: each read is
+    /// a port-I/O VM exit, and under WHP those are handled in user mode, so a
+    /// burst of them can take far longer than the hardware window and straddle
+    /// a second boundary. A guest that read `sec = 59` and then a minute that
+    /// had already ticked would land up to 59 seconds ahead — and if the tick
+    /// crossed midnight on 31 December, in the wrong year, which for
+    /// certificate validity is the whole problem again.
+    ///
+    /// Latching for a window rather than forever keeps the device a clock
+    /// rather than a snapshot: a later reader — a resume path re-reading the
+    /// persistent clock, or a guest polling for the seconds digit to advance —
+    /// still sees the host clock move. 100 ms is far longer than any plausible
+    /// read burst and far shorter than the one-second resolution the registers
+    /// can express, so it is invisible from the guest either way.
+    fn calendar(&mut self) -> Calendar {
+        let now = Instant::now();
+        match self.latch {
+            Some((sampled_at, calendar)) if now.duration_since(sampled_at) < LATCH_WINDOW => {
+                calendar
+            }
+            _ => {
+                let calendar = calendar_from_unix(get_time(ClockType::Real) / NANOS_PER_SECOND);
+                self.latch = Some((now, calendar));
+                calendar
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Serves one register, by index.
+    fn read_register(&mut self, index: u8) -> u8 {
+        match index {
+            REG_SECONDS => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().second),
+            REG_MINUTES => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().minute),
+            REG_HOURS => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().hour),
+            REG_WEEKDAY => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().weekday),
+            REG_DAY_OF_MONTH => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().day),
+            REG_MONTH => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().month),
+            REG_YEAR => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().year),
+            REG_CENTURY => bin_to_bcd(self.calendar().century),
+            REG_STATUS_A => STATUS_A,
+            REG_STATUS_B => STATUS_B,
+            REG_STATUS_C => STATUS_C,
+            REG_STATUS_D => STATUS_D,
+            // Everything else, including the three alarm registers, is CMOS
+            // RAM. The alarm registers read as zero because no alarm can be
+            // set: writes to the data port are not accepted (see `write`).
+            _ => self.data[(index & INDEX_MASK) as usize],
+        }
     }
 }
 
@@ -53,7 +293,7 @@ impl BusDevice for Cmos {
             }
             DATA_OFFSET => {
                 debug!("cmos: read data offset from index={:x}", self.index);
-                self.data[(self.index & INDEX_MASK) as usize]
+                self.read_register(self.index & INDEX_MASK)
             }
             _ => {
                 debug!("cmos: unsupported read offset");
@@ -73,7 +313,183 @@ impl BusDevice for Cmos {
                 debug!("cmos: update index");
                 self.index = data[0] & INDEX_MASK;
             }
+            // Writes to the data port stay ignored, so the time registers are
+            // read-only and track the host. Accepting them would mean modelling
+            // a guest-set offset from host time, and no guest libkrun boots asks
+            // for one: the x86_64 guest has no `CONFIG_RTC_CLASS`, hence no
+            // driver that could issue a `settimeofday`-to-RTC writeback, and the
+            // aarch64 guest talks to the PL031 instead.
             _ => debug!("cmos: ignoring unsupported write to CMOS"),
         }
     }
 }
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod tests {
+    use super::*;
+
+    /// Reads one register through the bus interface, the way a guest would.
+    fn read_reg(cmos: &mut Cmos, index: u8) -> u8 {
+        cmos.write(0, INDEX_OFFSET, &[index]);
+        let mut out = [0u8];
+        cmos.read(0, DATA_OFFSET, &mut out);
+        out[0]
+    }
+
+    fn bcd_to_bin(val: u8) -> u32 {
+        u32::from((val & 0x0f) + (val >> 4) * 10)
+    }
+
+    /// `mktime64()` from the Linux kernel (kernel/time/time.c), transcribed
+    /// verbatim, including its acceptance of month 0 and day 0. This is the
+    /// consumer of everything this device publishes, so the tests below check
+    /// the register file by running it through the real decoder rather than by
+    /// asserting on the encoding in isolation.
+    fn mktime64(year0: u32, mon0: u32, day: u32, hour: u32, min: u32, sec: u32) -> i64 {
+        let (mut mon, mut year) = (mon0 as i64, year0 as i64);
+        mon -= 2;
+        if mon <= 0 {
+            mon += 12;
+            year -= 1;
+        }
+        ((((year / 4 - year / 100 + year / 400 + 367 * mon / 12 + day as i64) + year * 365
+            - 719_499)
+            * 24
+            + hour as i64)
+            * 60
+            + min as i64)
+            * 60
+            + sec as i64
+    }
+
+    /// The number the defect produced, and the reason this device exists.
+    ///
+    /// A zeroed register file means year 0, month 0, day 0. Linux adds
+    /// `CMOS_YEARS_OFFS` to the year and hands the rest to `mktime64()`
+    /// unvalidated, which walks a month and a day back off the start of 2000
+    /// and lands on 1999-11-30T00:00:00Z. The guest that found this reported
+    /// `Tue Nov 30 00:02:19 UTC 1999` at an uptime of 139.62 s, which is this
+    /// constant plus that uptime, to the second.
+    #[test]
+    fn zeroed_register_file_is_1999_11_30() {
+        assert_eq!(mktime64(2000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0), 943_920_000);
+    }
+
+    /// Decodes a calendar exactly as `mach_get_cmos_time()` does with
+    /// `CONFIG_ACPI` off: BCD everywhere, no century register, year + 2000.
+    fn decode_as_guest(c: Calendar) -> i64 {
+        mktime64(
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.year)) + 2000,
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.month)),
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.day)),
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.hour)),
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.minute)),
+            bcd_to_bin(bin_to_bcd(c.second)),
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// Every date this century, encoded and then decoded by the guest's own
+    /// arithmetic, has to come back unchanged. The stride of 999_983 s is prime
+    /// and coprime with the day, so the walk lands on every phase of the day,
+    /// week, month and leap cycle, including 2024-02-29 and every month length.
+    ///
+    /// The range stops at 2100 because `CMOS_YEARS_OFFS` does: a kernel built
+    /// without `CONFIG_ACPI` has no century register to read, so two BCD digits
+    /// plus a hard-coded 2000 is all it can express. `known_dates` checks that
+    /// the century register itself keeps counting past that, for a guest that
+    /// does read one.
+    #[test]
+    fn round_trips_through_the_guest_decoder() {
+        let mut secs = 946_684_800u64; // 2000-01-01T00:00:00Z
+        while secs < 4_102_444_800 {
+            // 2100-01-01T00:00:00Z
+            let calendar = calendar_from_unix(secs);
+            assert_eq!(
+                decode_as_guest(calendar),
+                secs as i64,
+                "round trip failed at unix {secs}"
+            );
+            secs += 999_983;
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Spot-checks against dates computed independently, including the two the
+    /// investigation turned on.
+    #[test]
+    fn known_dates() {
+        // The host clock at the failing run: 2026-08-14T14:58:07Z, a Friday.
+        let c = calendar_from_unix(1_786_719_487);
+        assert_eq!(
+            (
+                c.century, c.year, c.month, c.day, c.hour, c.minute, c.second
+            ),
+            (20, 26, 8, 14, 14, 58, 7)
+        );
+        assert_eq!(c.weekday, 6); // Sunday = 1, so Friday = 6.
+
+        // The epoch itself: 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, a Thursday.
+        let c = calendar_from_unix(0);
+        assert_eq!((c.century, c.year, c.month, c.day), (19, 70, 1, 1));
+        assert_eq!(c.weekday, 5);
+
+        // A leap day, and the day after a century that is not a leap year.
+        assert_eq!(
+            (
+                calendar_from_unix(1_709_164_800).month,
+                calendar_from_unix(1_709_164_800).day
+            ),
+            (2, 29)
+        );
+        let c = calendar_from_unix(4_107_542_400);
+        assert_eq!((c.century, c.year, c.month, c.day), (21, 0, 3, 1));
+    }
+
+    /// The registers a guest actually reads must be BCD, must be stable across
+    /// one read burst, and must not be the zeros that started all this.
+    #[test]
+    fn register_file_publishes_a_plausible_present() {
+        let mut cmos = Cmos::new(512 * 1024 * 1024, 0);
+
+        // Status registers: UIP clear (or `mach_get_cmos_time()` spins for
+        // ever), 24-hour BCD, and the valid-time bit set.
+        assert_eq!(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_STATUS_A) & 0x80, 0);
+        assert_eq!(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_STATUS_B) & 0x06, 0x02);
+        assert_eq!(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_STATUS_D) & 0x80, 0x80);
+
+        // The whole burst, in the order `mach_get_cmos_time()` issues it.
+        let sec = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_SECONDS));
+        let min = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_MINUTES));
+        let hour = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_HOURS));
+        let day = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_DAY_OF_MONTH));
+        let mon = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_MONTH));
+        let year = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_YEAR));
+        let century = bcd_to_bin(read_reg(&mut cmos, REG_CENTURY));
+
+        assert!(sec < 60 && min < 60 && hour < 24);
+        assert!((1..=31).contains(&day) && (1..=12).contains(&mon));
+        assert_eq!(century, 20);
+
+        // What the guest computes must be within a minute of what the host
+        // clock says, and — the point of the exercise — nowhere near 1999.
+        let guest = mktime64(year + 2000, mon, day, hour, min, sec);
+        let host = (get_time(ClockType::Real) / NANOS_PER_SECOND) as i64;
+        assert!(
+            (guest - host).abs() < 60,
+            "guest would compute {guest}, host says {host}"
+        );
+        assert!(guest > 1_700_000_000, "guest would compute {guest}");
+    }
+
+    /// The memory-size bytes a BIOS-less guest may still look for are unchanged
+    /// by the RTC work, and the RAM area is untouched by it.
+    #[test]
+    fn memory_size_registers_survive() {
+        let mut cmos = Cmos::new(512 * 1024 * 1024, 4 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024);
+        let ext = u32::from(read_reg(&mut cmos, 0x34)) | u32::from(read_reg(&mut cmos, 0x35)) << 8;
+        assert_eq!(ext, (512 - 16) * 1024 * 1024 / (64 * 1024));
+        let high = u32::from(read_reg(&mut cmos, 0x5b))
+            | u32::from(read_reg(&mut cmos, 0x5c)) << 8
+            | u32::from(read_reg(&mut cmos, 0x5d)) << 16;
+        assert_eq!(u64::from(high), 4 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 / (64 * 1024));
+    }
+}
